1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:19,152
The school reading names the NCSBN Clinical Judgment Measurement
Model. Its six functions are recognizing cues, analyzing them,
prioritizing hypotheses, generating solutions, taking action and
evaluating outcomes. They form a loop because new information can
change the priority.

2
00:00:19,152 --> 00:00:36,552
This framework was developed to describe nursing judgment in a testing
and teaching context. Borrow its questions, not its professional
authority. For a PCP, current BCEHS pathways, licence, training and
service policy still govern each action.

3
00:00:36,552 --> 00:00:50,616
Autonomy is not improvisation. State what you think is happening, then
locate the current clinical pathway that fits the presentation. Check
the page heading and revision context before relying on a saved
printout or memory.

4
00:00:50,616 --> 00:01:08,808
Read the provider-level rows separately. An action listed for ACP or
CCP does not become a PCP action because it seems logical. Follow the
PCP row, required consultation and linked pathway; if uncertainty
affects safety, ask early.

5
00:01:08,808 --> 00:01:26,520
A slow pulse can be tolerated by one patient and dangerous in another.
Pair the number with symptoms, perfusion, rhythm and the person’s
baseline. A single normal blood pressure does not cancel a worsening
level of consciousness or cool, poorly perfused skin.

6
00:01:26,520 --> 00:01:40,464
Use a consistent sequence for reassessment and record the time. Trend
vital signs with the patient’s appearance and response. Do not wait
for a scheduled interval when the patient deteriorates or a new threat
appears.

7
00:01:40,464 --> 00:01:56,568
A 66-year-old feels suddenly weak and nauseated after returning from
dialysis. The pulse is slow, the skin is cool and the monitor shows a
new conduction abnormality. The patient is awake but says standing
makes the dizziness worse.

8
00:01:56,568 --> 00:02:14,400
This is an original practice case, not the school case or an answer
key. Begin with immediate threats and perfusion. Ask what changed,
obtain the actual medication history and ECG context, and consider
several reversible causes before selecting a pathway.

9
00:02:14,400 --> 00:02:30,960
An AV block is a delay or interruption in electrical conduction from
atria to ventricles. The pattern on the ECG matters; a slow pulse
alone does not identify the block type or its cause. Relate the
tracing to symptoms and perfusion.

10
00:02:30,960 --> 00:02:48,384
Keep the facts in one column and hypotheses in another. Recent
dialysis raises a relevant clinical context, but it does not prove an
electrolyte problem. Medication exposure, ischemia, hypoxia and other
causes remain possible until assessed.

11
00:02:48,384 --> 00:03:04,608
BCEHS bradycardia guidance emphasizes the overall patient and
reversible causes. Ask about prescribed and non-prescribed medicines,
recent dose changes, missed or duplicate doses, dialysis timing and
possible co-ingestions.

12
00:03:04,608 --> 00:03:24,240
Beta-blocker toxicity may include bradycardia, hypotension, altered
mental status or respiratory effects, but no single clue proves an
overdose. Check airway, oxygenation, ventilation and glucose as
indicated, consider co-ingestion, and use the current pathway.

13
00:03:24,240 --> 00:03:42,792
In the case, say aloud: “This patient has symptomatic bradycardia with
a new conduction change and poor perfusion cues. I am starting the
current pathway, preparing early conveyance and requesting additional
clinical resources.” That is a reasoned, time-aware plan.

14
00:03:42,792 --> 00:04:01,440
Follow the current PCP-level C02 and J07 actions. Request ACP support
when indicated. Do not delay conveyance to complete a perfect history,
and do not independently add higher-level medicines or procedures
because the ECG looks concerning.

15
00:04:01,440 --> 00:04:16,320
Reassessment is not a final check performed only before arrival. It
begins as soon as an action is taken. Compare the response with the
expected outcome and return to an earlier reasoning step if the
response is absent or unexpected.

16
00:04:16,320 --> 00:04:31,632
In the case, increasing confusion or worsening skin perfusion would
increase urgency even if the monitor still shows a similar rate. Say
what changed, what you think it means and what new resource or
transport action follows.

17
00:04:31,632 --> 00:04:44,688
Heart failure is a syndrome, not a single pump measurement. Reduced-
ejection and preserved-ejection phenotypes describe different
mechanisms, but neither label can be assigned from a roadside symptom
alone.

18
00:04:44,688 --> 00:05:02,472
Left-sided pressure can back up toward the lungs; right-sided pressure
can back up into systemic veins. The conditions can overlap.
Breathlessness, orthopnea, crackles, edema or neck-vein findings are
clues to interpret together, not a diagnosis by checklist.

19
00:05:02,472 --> 00:05:19,056
A patient with known heart failure is now unable to lie flat and has
stopped halfway to the bathroom. A caregiver reports that the
medication pack changed recently. The patient also has ankle swelling,
but the cause of today’s deterioration is not yet known.

20
00:05:19,056 --> 00:05:40,608
Compare with baseline breathing, activity, weight history if
available, medication timing, chest symptoms, infection clues and
rhythm concerns. Use the relevant current BCEHS respiratory/cardiac
pathway. Do not infer that all breathlessness is fluid overload or
that a visible edema pattern settles treatment.

21
00:05:40,608 --> 00:05:57,912
An 82-year-old woman trips on a curb and lands on her side. She takes
an anticoagulant, is unsure whether her head struck the pavement and
has a painful forearm deformity. A bruise over the hip is becoming
larger while the crew gathers history.

22
00:05:57,912 --> 00:06:15,696
This is an original scenario, not the school stair-fall case. Reassess
for visible and concealed bleeding, head injury, perfusion change and
pain. The medication increases concern; it does not reveal whether
bleeding is present or how severe it is.

23
00:06:15,696 --> 00:06:33,600
Current BCEHS D02 emphasizes rapid control of external hemorrhage,
recognition of occult bleeding, heat-loss prevention and appropriate
trauma conveyance. A patient taking an anticoagulant deserves careful
assessment even when the external wound looks small.

24
00:06:33,600 --> 00:06:49,488
A normal early blood pressure or calm appearance does not rule out
internal bleeding. Record repeated findings and changes. Follow
current trauma pathways and do not use textbook hemorrhage classes as
a measured estimate of this patient’s blood loss.

25
00:06:49,488 --> 00:07:05,784
Before splinting, describe what you can actually assess beyond the
injury. Compare with the opposite limb when appropriate. Record a
finding as “unable to test” when pain, swelling, altered consciousness
or injury prevents a reliable check.

26
00:07:05,784 --> 00:07:24,528
After an authorized splint or movement, repeat the same checks and
record the time. A newly absent pulse, new sensory change or colder,
duskier skin is urgent. Fracture reduction is not a student action
unless the current authority and direct training explicitly permit it.

27
00:07:24,528 --> 00:07:42,000
Polypharmacy means that several medicines are used together; the list
alone does not prove an adverse effect. Ask what was changed, who
changed it, when the last dose was taken and whether the patient uses
a weekly pack, patch, inhaler or non-prescription product.

28
00:07:42,000 --> 00:07:57,552
A recently added medicine may contribute to dizziness, bleeding risk,
sedation or a change in breathing, but the patient may also have a new
illness. Attribute each history item to its source and keep the
clinical differential open.

29
00:07:57,552 --> 00:08:14,352
Mood disorders can affect energy, sleep, appetite, attention and daily
function. A patient may describe depression or a period of unusually
elevated or irritable mood, but a paramedic encounter does not
establish a psychiatric diagnosis.

30
00:08:14,352 --> 00:08:33,552
Do not treat an unusual mood or behavior as proof that symptoms are
psychiatric. Ask about immediate safety and self-harm concerns with
calm, direct language when indicated, assess for medical or
toxicologic causes, and use the current service pathway for support
and conveyance.

31
00:08:33,552 --> 00:08:45,744
Separate the patient’s own words from what you observed. Include a
timeline, the source of medication information, the latest distal
neurovascular check and how it changed after movement, when relevant.

32
00:08:45,744 --> 00:09:15,552
For consultation, organize the specific request using SBAR. For a
current BCEHS care transition, A zero three specifies ATMIST AMBO. In
either setting, name your leading concern and the alternatives not
excluded. Give actual actions, response, trend and remaining tasks,
then confirm who accepted responsibility. A report recited to an
unavailable or unsuitable receiver is not a completed handover.

33
00:09:15,552 --> 00:09:49,728
Return to our patient with slow pulse, poor perfusion and a recent
dialysis history. The cumulative reading now brings the wider pathway
families into this same reasoning task. A plausible potassium problem,
medicine effect or conduction disorder is a hypothesis, not a measured
result. Treat the immediate airway, breathing and perfusion needs
while obtaining the relevant history, monitoring and current
authorized guidance. Parallel assessment matters more than finding one
diagnosis that explains every clue.

34
00:09:49,728 --> 00:10:21,816
Use the reader by clinical round rather than memorizing a list of
codes. Each round connects recognition, an authorized action, a check
of its effect and a transfer of responsibility. Earlier detailed
explanations remain in the same reader; newly added rounds cover the
previously thin domains. After a round, close the text and explain
what would change your next action in this patient. Naming the pathway
without explaining that decision is not the learning objective.

35
00:10:21,816 --> 00:10:58,320
If this patient becomes confused, check glucose as indicated without
abandoning the primary assessment. Hypoglycemia can impair cerebral
function; safe treatment depends on the patient, route and current
provider guidance. Oral treatment is not suitable for someone who
cannot safely swallow. The current adult E01 intravenous option uses
D10W, ten to twenty-five grams, equivalent to one hundred to two
hundred fifty millilitres, with access checks and reassessment. This
is adult pathway context, not a child or newborn order.

36
00:10:58,320 --> 00:11:38,040
A normal-looking glucose does not exclude SGLT2-associated
ketoacidosis, and a better glucose does not establish that a patient
can remain home. The reader explains every adult E01 nonconveyance
condition, including cause, recovery, exclusions, supervision and
follow-up. Ask separately about adrenal or pituitary disease and
interrupted steroid treatment. E02 requires its actual eligibility and
an existing prescription, with PCP consultation and route rules; an
EMR collecting the patient's medicine is not permission to prepare or
administer it.

37
00:11:38,040 --> 00:12:12,384
In the bradycardia case, relate the rhythm to perfusion and possible
causes. In another patient, a fast sinus rate may be compensation for
shock rather than a rhythm to suppress. A narrow fast tracing is not
automatically an accessory-pathway disorder. Similarly, stable angina
is not the same as acute coronary syndrome, and STEMI versus NSTEMI is
not a reliable complete-versus-partial blockage rule. The useful
question is how the actual presentation changes the current
assessment, destination and authorized support.

38
00:12:12,384 --> 00:12:48,696
Mean arterial pressure is an estimate of systemic driving pressure,
not proof of adequate coronary, cerebral or limb perfusion. Use it
with the patient and the trend. For shock, edema or a rhythm problem,
distinguish oxygenation, ventilation and circulation rather than
adding one intervention to every presentation. Read any synchronized-
cardioversion, mechanical-ventilation or advanced medication detail in
its own provider context; an adjacent table or a mistaken cross-
reference does not extend a PCP's authority.

39
00:12:48,696 --> 00:13:25,224
Return to our anticoagulated fall patient. A visible limb injury can
distract from intracranial bleeding, chest injury or hidden blood
loss. Assess the whole patient and repeat findings, not just pain at
the obvious site. Before and after authorized movement or splinting,
document distal sensation, movement and perfusion. A pulse can remain
present in compartment syndrome; waiting for every classic sign can
miss a developing threat. A normal early observation is a baseline for
comparison, not a discharge guarantee.

40
00:13:25,224 --> 00:13:58,536
Adult NEXUS criteria do not clear every spinal region or every patient
group, and an Ottawa ankle rule requires its full pain-zone and
examination context. Neither rule replaces treatment of the immediate
threat. Chest-wound care must follow the actual current device and
pathway rather than blending vented-seal guidance with older three-
sided-dressing wording. For burns, cool the injury without cooling the
whole patient, and separate burn size estimation from an advanced
resuscitation prescription.

41
00:13:58,536 --> 00:14:38,256
When the story includes a heater, enclosed space, chemical or
collapsed coworker, reassess scene safety before becoming another
patient. A tactical cold zone and a chemical decontamination zone are
different systems. Ordinary pulse oximetry can look reassuring in
carbon monoxide poisoning; a later low carbon monoxide measurement
also needs exposure and oxygen-treatment context. Odor is not a
reliable cyanide test, and hydrogen sulfide can prevent oxygen use at
the cellular level. Appropriate rescue, decontamination and specialist
support depend on the actual hazard.

42
00:14:38,256 --> 00:15:17,856
For medicine ingestion, identify the actual product, immediate or
modified release, possible co-ingestants and time course. An initially
well patient is not automatically safe after a beta blocker or
calcium-channel blocker exposure. Tricyclic poisoning uses a QRS risk
threshold that is not simply the adult wide-complex definition.
Cholinergic poisoning can combine secretions with muscle weakness;
treating muscarinic effects does not itself reverse every nicotinic
effect. Use the relevant current pathway and consultation, not a
memorized antidote attached to an uncertain label.

43
00:15:17,856 --> 00:15:56,616
Heatstroke is a dangerous clinical syndrome involving central nervous
system dysfunction in a heat context; do not require absent sweating
or wait for a single magic temperature. Use safe prompt cooling and
support through the current pathway; antipyretics do not correct
heatstroke physiology. For a diver, decompression illness and arterial
gas embolism may overlap. Avoid excluding them by one absolute onset
rule. Oxygen, specialist coordination, transport and any pressure-
exposure decisions require the actual diving pathway, not an
improvised return underwater.

44
00:15:56,616 --> 00:16:34,176
Infection control depends on route of spread, exposure and current
precautions; an old age cutoff, immunity shortcut or fixed room-
clearance time is not a universal rule. For a low-acuity referral,
read every condition rather than treating a reassuring score as
permission to leave. The reader distinguishes the older selected NEWS
referral conditions and the contradictory minor-allergy referral
wording. A capable refusal is legally and clinically different from
meeting an approved alternative-care pathway. Record unresolved risk
and who accepts the ongoing plan.

45
00:16:34,176 --> 00:16:47,184
When the situation changes, move through the judgment loop again. The
best response is not the most complicated one; it is the safest action
that fits the patient, the current pathway and your authorized role.

46
00:16:47,184 --> 00:17:03,360
Next, practise the school’s complex-scenario block. You will use
incomplete histories, two-patient prioritization, daily PAA review and
a shared debrief. The block revisits existing skills rather than
adding a new skill station.
